# Runbook: manage users

Users live in the Cognito user pool `{{ project_slug }}-<env>`. Sign-up is
invite-only by default.

Find the pool ID:

```bash
aws cloudformation describe-stacks --stack-name {{ project_slug }}-backend-dev \
  --query "Stacks[0].Outputs[?OutputKey=='UserPoolId'].OutputValue" --output text
```

## Invite a user

```bash
aws cognito-idp admin-create-user --user-pool-id <pool-id> \
  --username person@example.com \
  --user-attributes Name=email,Value=person@example.com Name=email_verified,Value=true \
  --desired-delivery-mediums EMAIL
```

Cognito emails a temporary password; the user sets a new one at first sign-in.

## Make someone an admin

```bash
aws cognito-idp admin-add-user-to-group --user-pool-id <pool-id> \
  --username person@example.com --group-name admin
```

The change applies at their next sign-in (the group is in the token).

## Remove access

```bash
aws cognito-idp admin-disable-user --user-pool-id <pool-id> --username person@example.com
aws cognito-idp admin-user-global-sign-out --user-pool-id <pool-id> --username person@example.com
```

Existing access tokens stay valid until they expire (at most one hour).

## Allow self sign-up

Set `AllowAdminCreateUserOnly: false` in `apps/backend/infra/auth.yaml` and deploy.
Consider bot protection (Cognito threat protection) before opening sign-up publicly.

## Emails in production

Cognito's default sender is limited to a few emails per day. Before launch, configure
Amazon SES as the user pool's email sender (`EmailConfiguration` in `auth.yaml`).
